**Definition 163.1 (Attribution of the six-active synthesis).** The
complete projection, joint endpoint lifts and their continuum synthesis
are repository-derived mathematical statements. The first thirteen score
primitives and Theorem 117.2 are credited in Section 156; the coefficient
criterion, exact original transport, residual placement and complement
are Theorems 109.1, 110.2, 113.2 and 113.3. The law obstruction of
Theorem 112.2 concerns preservation of one $N$ law, and is compatible
with replacement by a different $O$ law at the same cap.

The mature interpretation of linear projection as a multiple-objective
linear problem is [Löhne and Weißing, *Equivalence between polyhedral
projection, multiple objective linear programming and vector linear
programming*, arXiv:1507.00228v5, problem PP and Theorem 3](https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.00228v5).
It supplies that interpretation, not the particular 75-row geometry or
these simultaneous lifts. [Kaniewski, Lee and de Wolf, *Query complexity
in expectation*, arXiv:1411.7280v1, Definition 8 and Theorem 9](https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.7280v1)
concerns scalar nonnegative-output expectation complexity, not one
normalized seven-label probability table or first-zero stopping.
[Fritz, Gonda, Perrone and Rischel, *Representable Markov categories and
comparison of statistical experiments in categorical probability*,
arXiv:2010.07416v3, Corollary 5.15](https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.07416v3)
uses a conditionals-bearing, almost-sure compatibly representable Markov
category and a discrete hypothesis object relative to a prior; it does
not supply this uniform actual-source hard-count comparison. These are
attributed intermediate viewpoints, not additional acquisition theorems
or assertions of exhaustive novelty or literary priority.

